  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a detoxification concept in which two hazardous chemicals are converted into safer products using a light-driven MOF catalyst in solution. On the left, the compounds labeled CEES and DMNP are identified as toxic. Their chemical structures are shown to represent a sulfur mustard simulant and an organophosphate nerve-agent simulant.\n\nAt the bottom, a simple reaction setup is depicted: a vial containing a buffer solution with suspended MOF catalyst particles is irradiated by a light source. Curved arrows connect the toxic compounds to the products on the right, indicating that illumination triggers catalytic transformation in the liquid phase.\n\nOn the right, the resulting compounds are labeled non-toxic. CEES appears to be oxidized to sulfoxide-type products, while DMNP is broken down into dimethyl phosphate and a nitrophenol-like aromatic product. The overall message is that the MOF material, under light in a buffered medium, can simultaneously neutralize chemically distinct threats through different reaction pathways: oxidation for the sulfur-containing agent and hydrolysis or bond cleavage for the phosphate ester. The color transition from the left panel to the right reinforces the change from hazardous starting materials to safer end products.",
